From: "Kobielus, James" <KobielJ@usva8.dyncorp.com>
Date: Tue, 01 Nov 94 09:02:00 PST
Subject: [#94-11-2] Szabo's Teleprogramming Protocol

To Nick Szabo:

I was fascinated by your idea, in the 3Q94 Extropy journal, for a 
teleprogramming protocol that "will be used to reduce the time-lag problem 
[in interplanetary missions] to a lag in transmitting corrections to 
simulation errors."  It addresses the fact that space probes, with or 
without human passengers, must be autonomous agents with very long 
control/feedback loops.  Earth-bound mission "controllers" will try to 
anticipate, simulate, and control for "simultaneous" conditions on which 
there is lagged confirmation.  Each party in the interstellar transaction 
would maintain three parallel models of the other transactant:  its 
condition when it transmitted the signal I am currently receiving, its 
condition "now", and its condition when it receives the signal that I am 
currently transmitting.  Communications would be negotiations--rather than 
master/slave transactions--among autonomous agents.

Your idea indirectly points up the fact that all space travelers will have 
to carry with them a simulated virtual earth--climate, culture, 
companions--to overcome the lonesome void.  We would transmit broad 
parametric corrections to each others' virtual earths for major new trends 
and developments, as well as continuous streams of multimedia news.  There 
would always be at least one surface in every habitable space environment to 
display the news and earth simulations.  The surface would be visually 
"aged" to indicate the current loop length to earth.

We also need to explore a sort of telecoordination protocol for linking 
people across the high lonesome of inner space.  Computer bulletin boards, 
discussion groups, and mailservers allow us to interact exclusively with 
virtual representations of each other.  Each of us is simulating the others 
in his or her own minds.  Usually, our simulations are projections of our 
own egos and prejudices as much as interpretations of what the others have 
actually communicated.

Maybe we could have a content analyzer that continually assesses and 
displays distance indicators in on-line conversations, such as 
confirmations, gainsaying, rebuttals, name calling, and ad hominem attacks. 
 It could also have decision support features, such as challenging me to 
respond to loose threads that will make or break further relations, or 
prompting me to abandon a conversation that's going nowhere.

Much of my time since childhood has been spent transmitting corrections to 
other people's errors simulating me.  The void is everywhere and loops are 
long.

Jim Kobielus

