At 06:34 PM 10/31/94 -0500, cactus@bb.hks.net (L. Todd Masco) wrote:
>In article <941031223830_70544.1227_CHV73-2@CompuServe.COM>,
>Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com> wrote:
>>L. Todd Masco wrote 10/30/94: <...our thoughts and feelings are the result
>>of a machine evolved to consider complex situations...>
>>
>>I am not a machine.  Enlightenment determinism is a dried-out husk of
>>ideational detritus, yuck, ick and pew.
>
>Don't misunderstand me.  We are exceedinly complex machines and by no
>means deterministic. [use] "system,"[?]

Hey, no need to be ashamed of the word "machine"; it describes the
finest guns, computers, programs, and organisms around, as well as
notional ones of even greater elegance and complexity.

Reilly is, as far as anyone can tell, an elaborate machine that, among
other functions, implements the Reilly Jones personality and its
attendant enterprises. If you want to say "I don't feel like a machine",
or "I feel diminished and threatened by the thought that I may be 'only'
a machine", well, we could address those questions psychologically, if
we had a psychologist (do we?).

This is getting dangerously close to the free will[1] thread from a year
or more ago, and I don't care for another round of philosophy 101, so
suffice it to say we are not leaping back to "enlightenment determinism"
or other pre-QM, pre-cybernetic naivete, but depending on robust 21st
century pragmatism.

F'rinstance, I never have had patience with AI philosophy debates,
although those who have the wit and the stomach are welcome to them,
my answer being, let's build the damned machines and ask them. We're
going to build the best AIs we can anyway, so proofs that we can't
are not germane.

[1] We believe in free will; we have no choice in the matter!



