Date: 01 Nov 94 15:03:55 EST
Subject: [#94-11-7] Zen Physics Enlightenment

Anders Sandberg wrote 11/1/94: <Personally I regard mysticism as useful even to
others than the mystic, because from time to time their insights can be
expressed and used in other fields. What we need is simply a better way to link
the mystic and practical worlds.>

These insights are what I was referring to as fruits of long-jump searches in
knowledge space.  It doesn't happen very often, but there have been new vistas
opened up for human experiences to travel to, by mystics throughout history.

This linkage is an excellent idea.  Another type of cognitive bootstrap besides
the semantic bootstrap.  The semantic bootstrap is too closely linked to
conceptual categorization which, by its nature, abstracts too much for the
implicit knowledge in the mystic state.  A neuronic bypass of abstraction, say
between the hippocampal region and Broca's and Wernicke's areas that doesn't
take part in the field processing of value-category memory in the neocortex to
avoid entanglement in desires.  But how to bring it out, so others can visualize
it?  It is essentially the irrational part of the universe, energy field
fluctuations, without the abstract approximation of any wave equations.  A
computer cannot adequately model and represent the field fluxes, because: 1) the
fluxes are transcomputable and 2) the computer cannot model the subjective
consciousness' dynamic interactions within the larger non-self field.  By
'adequate' I just mean it would pale before the real experience of it.

Bonaventure described this energy field: "In this immeasurable and absolute
elevation of soul, forgetting all created things and liberated from them, thou
shalt rise above thyself and beyond all creation to find thyself within the
shaft of light that flashes out from the divine, mysterious darkness."

That's about the most that can be said about the energy field without actually
falling back into semantic approximations and interpretations of the field,
which fall far short of what is actually there.

