On Fri, 28 Oct 1994, Kobielus, James wrote:

> I know it's not fun to have your basic assumptions challenged.  If you want 
> to expand your influence and accomplish your objectives, though, you'll have 
> to welcome--and respond clearly and nondefensively--to new posters.  If you 
> remain an insular, condescending, know-it-all clique, your leverage will 
> never grow.

I don't think anyone here hasn't really responded clearly and nondefensively
postings which question the "basic assumptions". The point is that most
consider them "basic assumptions" which have been quite literally thrashed
out for several years on this list, and which don't really generate any
new ideas. The arguments, both for and against, tend to be a waste of
bandwidth.

> Did it ever occur to the Extropians that most government agencies operate in 
> a quasi-free-market mode, with lone champions developing business plans, 
> building projects, scrounging up the financing, organizing coalitions, 
> producing products and services, serving customers, and amassing resources 
> to achieve their objectives?  Has any of you ever worked in a government 
> bureaucracy?

As any libertarian or anarcho-whatever will say, governments work through
coercion. Everything they do can't be said to work in a free market, 
because the source of their capital is based on extortion. Simple as
that. If it did work on a free-market principle, I don't believe we'd
have such organizations as the NSA, or most of the military, or for that
matter much of the regulation currently in place by the FDA. NASA would
quite likely not exist (in any recognizable form...)

And in my opinion, all of these things would be a good thing...

> Government agencies can be a great instrument for achieving leverage. 

At what cost? And could it (whatever "it" is) be done better without
the government.

>  They're no more inherently sinister and corrupt than private 
> corporations--probably less so, since most of what they do is visible to 
> most of the populace.  The "free market" is at least as coercive--setting 

The fact that the government can put me in jail for not giving them
money, while a corporation can't, is a huge difference.

> standards, constraining our choices, cutting off support for outdated 
> products, tightening the intellectual property screws, polluting and gouging 
> the environment--as any government.

I disagree, since a free-market, even a limited free market which most
of the world enjoys, offers far more choice in products and services
which are offered than anything any government has ever offered. Witness
the former communist Russia (and the proliferation of consumer goods now
that the "iron curtain" has lifted...)

> Government has to be responsible--or at least has to respond--to most of the 
> people most of the time.  More and more government agencies are being 

This is by definition mob-rule and something we seem to have a lot of
in democratric countries...

> quasi-privatized as a way of improving efficiency and service.  That's for 
> the best.  It combines the great sweep, clout, and legitimacy with the 
> flexibility of private initiative.

Actually I attribute this mainly to the fact that so much money is spent
on activities such as the military and many short sighted, badly planned
social programs. It's becoming harder and harder (and more and more
expensive) to pay for the whims of the voting populace. I'm hoping this
eventually will smother governments under the cost of misguided social
programs, but I doubt it...

> Initiative--and who's willing to exercise it over the long haul--will make 
> or break us in the long term on the road to boundless expansion.

Amazing though how the government still continues to try and stifle
innovation and experimentation. I'm sure we can all come up with
examples of this...

I don't mind having basic assumptions questioned, but you still have yet
to give concrete examples (unlike many of the excellent books in the
infamous reading list) of how governments (especially large bureaucratic
ones - is there any other kind?) can be "good" things. I've seen way
more evidence to the contrary, although I don't have any concrete proof
to offer, besides historical aberations, of societies where a
central ruling body didn't exist. This of course doesn't prove they
can't work... I'm still not convinced. Try again...

